--TEST--
Two doubles of one interface share a class and keep separate call records, and a partial records the calls it delegates as well as the ones it answers — `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`, `rule:testing/doubles`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The record is per *object*, which is the question a shared class raises:
// two doubles of one interface built the same way are one descriptor — that is
// what keeps the descriptor table O(call sites) — so if the ledger lived
// anywhere but in the instance's own slot, the second double would inherit the
// first one's calls and every assertion below would answer about the wrong
// subject.
interface Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool;
    public function purge(): void;
}

Mailer $first = Core\Test::double<Mailer>({
    send: fn(string $to): bool => true,
    purge: fn(): void => {},
});
Mailer $second = Core\Test::double<Mailer>({
    send: fn(string $to): bool => true,
    purge: fn(): void => {},
});

echo $first->send("ada@example.test") ? "sent" : "refused", "\n";

// 1. The call reached the double it was made on, and no other.
Core\Test::assertCalled($first, Mailer::send, {times: 1});
Core\Test::assertNeverCalled($second, Mailer::send);
echo "one record each\n";

// 2. A partial records both halves of what it does: the method its shape
// answers and the method it forwards to `$real` are one ledger, because what
// is being asserted about is the call and not which body took it.
final class RealMailer implements Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool {
        return true;
    }

    public function purge(): void {}
}

Mailer $half = Core\Test::partial<Mailer>(new RealMailer(), {
    purge: fn(): void => {},
});
echo $half->send("bob@example.test") ? "sent" : "refused", "\n";
$half->purge();

Core\Test::assertCalled($half, Mailer::send, {with: ["bob@example.test"]});
Core\Test::assertCalled($half, Mailer::purge, {times: 1});
echo "a delegated call is recorded too\n";
--EXPECT--
sent
one record each
sent
a delegated call is recorded too
